iefs of people in the Huarochirí Province of the Western Andes. Other pre-Inca cultures include Chavín, Paracas, Moche, and the Nazca culture.","evidence":"documented","universe":"inca-mythology","aliases":["Inca mythology"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":null,"image_source":null,"image_credit":null,"image_license":null,"image_usage":null,"image_alt":null},"connections":[{"id":"6c07c87e-25a5-4b8a-a17d-e025b40abee2","relationship":"part_of","title":"Part of","explanation":"Part of","strength":"0.9000","discovery_score":"0.8500","direction":"incoming","from_entity_id":"49f8a053-df44-4938-90bc-e25eb50dd076","to_entity_id":"3a9ee5fc-2495-4151-ae62-7db567836de9","entity_id":"49f8a053-df44-4938-90bc-e25eb50dd076","slug":"amaru-mythology","name":"Amaru","type":"topic","short_description":"In the mythology of the Andean civilizations of South America, the amaru or katari (Aymara) is a mythical serpent or dragon. In Inca mythology, Amaru is a huge double-headed serpent that dwells underground, at the…","evidence":"fiction","image_url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/AmaruMitoPeru.jpg/180px-AmaruMitoPeru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","path":"/inca-mythology/amaru-mythology","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic"}],"rabbit_holes":[],"copyright":{"theme":"inca-mythology","title":"Inca mythology is not owned by Ton-o-Lore.","body":"Inca mythology, related names, marks, and materials are the property of Public-domain traditions / respective modern rights holders. Ton-o-Lore is an independent educational encyclopedia project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by those rights holders. Identification marks, when shown, are low-resolution images used under a fair-use rationale for commentary and identification only — not free or redistributable artwork."},"subject":{"id":"inca-mythology","name":"Inca mythology","theme":"inca-mythology","path":"/inca-mythology","logo":null,"categories":["mythology"]}}